1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,371
The message says stop. Pip has already moved.

2
00:00:03,541 --> 00:00:10,901
Our simulated delivery rover received a message that was useful
when it was sent, but late when it arrived.

3
00:00:11,071 --> 00:00:16,703
A strong Wi-Fi signal does not tell us how quickly the whole
task will finish.

4
00:00:16,873 --> 00:00:21,119
Have you ever wondered why a fast connection can still feel
slow?

5
00:00:21,289 --> 00:00:28,222
Chapter four, Networking, gives us the measurements. Chapter
five, Applications, connects them to deadlines.

6
00:00:30,933 --> 00:00:36,287
Today you will distinguish bandwidth, throughput, latency,
jitter, and packet loss.

7
00:00:36,457 --> 00:00:41,983
You will use a numerical example to connect delay with a
physical consequence.

8
00:00:42,153 --> 00:00:46,441
You will choose responses such as compression, fewer round
trips,

9
00:00:46,551 --> 00:00:50,305
buffering, or local control for specific problems.

10
00:00:50,475 --> 00:00:54,998
Welcome to the tutorial on network challenges by Blue Impulse.

11
00:00:55,168 --> 00:00:59,584
Keep a calculator or paper ready. Try the delay calculation

12
00:00:59,694 --> 00:01:03,577
before checking the worked answer in the tutorial PDF.

13
00:01:06,411 --> 00:01:14,390
In the subtopic Capacity is not responsiveness, bandwidth
describes how much data a link can carry per second.

14
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:21,173
Throughput is the successful transfer rate actually achieved. It
can be lower than the link's capacity.

15
00:01:21,343 --> 00:01:27,743
Latency is how long a transfer or response takes. Jitter is
variation in that delay.

16
00:01:27,913 --> 00:01:31,774
Packet loss means some packets do not arrive as expected.

17
00:01:31,944 --> 00:01:37,107
These measures answer different questions. A connection can
carry a lot of data

18
00:01:37,217 --> 00:01:39,884
and still take a long time to respond.

19
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:43,744
When describing an experiment, specify what you measured:

20
00:01:43,854 --> 00:01:48,334
one-way delay, round-trip delay, or complete task response time.

21
00:01:51,802 --> 00:01:57,989
Imagine a road. A wider road can carry more cars at once, like
greater bandwidth.

22
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:02,554
How many cars actually arrive each second is closer to
throughput.

23
00:02:02,724 --> 00:02:09,252
How long one journey takes is closer to latency. Uneven arrival
times illustrate jitter,

24
00:02:09,362 --> 00:02:12,007
while missing deliveries illustrate loss.

25
00:02:12,177 --> 00:02:18,129
Widening a road does not shorten the distance to the destination
or remove every queue.

26
00:02:18,299 --> 00:02:23,355
Likewise, increasing link capacity does not erase propagation
time,

27
00:02:23,465 --> 00:02:27,966
server processing, or unnecessary back-and-forth exchanges.

28
00:02:28,136 --> 00:02:35,347
The analogy helps separate the ideas, but measure the actual
network before deciding which factor dominates.

29
00:02:35,517 --> 00:02:40,765
A road can be wide and still contain a long traffic-light delay.
That is why

30
00:02:40,875 --> 00:02:46,528
a capacity figure cannot by itself predict a small message's
arrival time.

31
00:02:50,759 --> 00:02:55,281
Now connect performance metrics to transmission media and
contention.

32
00:02:55,451 --> 00:03:02,513
Wireless devices may share a medium, compete for transmission
time, and experience interference.

33
00:03:02,683 --> 00:03:09,957
Heavy demand can create queues. Retransmissions after loss can
use more capacity and add delay.

34
00:03:10,127 --> 00:03:17,103
Modern switched full-duplex Ethernet should not be described as
suffering the old shared-hub collision model.

35
00:03:17,273 --> 00:03:23,929
The useful diagnosis depends on the network technology, the
traffic, and the end-to-end path.

36
00:03:24,099 --> 00:03:28,558
Try testing near the access point, with fewer competing
transfers,

37
00:03:28,668 --> 00:03:32,764
and at different times before blaming one advertised number.

38
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:45,409
In the TCP and IP subtopic, IP routes packets, while TCP can
provide an ordered, reliable byte stream.

39
00:03:45,579 --> 00:03:51,851
Reliability can require acknowledgements and retransmission, so
recovery may add delay.

40
00:03:52,021 --> 00:03:55,413
A transport choice does not by itself guarantee an

41
00:03:55,523 --> 00:04:00,088
application deadline or protect every message from an attacker.

42
00:04:00,258 --> 00:04:03,159
Some applications prefer timely delivery

43
00:04:03,269 --> 00:04:09,221
and handle missing data themselves. Others need complete,
correctly ordered transfer.

44
00:04:09,391 --> 00:04:16,197
Read the application's requirements before selecting a protocol
or interpreting a performance measurement.

45
00:04:16,367 --> 00:04:24,879
The security subtopics distinguish confidentiality, integrity,
and availability from simply having a fast link.

46
00:04:28,846 --> 00:04:33,347
For this calculation, switch to an illustrative vehicle
travelling at

47
00:04:33,457 --> 00:04:38,321
a constant thirty metres per second. This is not Pip's campus
speed.

48
00:04:38,491 --> 00:04:46,214
Distance travelled during delay equals speed multiplied by
delay. First convert milliseconds into seconds.

49
00:04:46,384 --> 00:04:54,618
Five hundred milliseconds is zero point five seconds. Thirty
times zero point five gives fifteen metres.

50
00:04:54,788 --> 00:05:01,423
Fifty milliseconds is zero point zero five seconds. That gives
one point five metres.

51
00:05:01,593 --> 00:05:09,145
Five milliseconds is zero point zero zero five seconds. That
gives zero point one five metres.

52
00:05:09,315 --> 00:05:16,398
Watch the three distances grow from the same starting point. We
changed delay while keeping speed fixed.

53
00:05:16,568 --> 00:05:24,205
These are distances before a response begins, not complete
stopping distances and not safety guarantees.

54
00:05:24,375 --> 00:05:26,658
Sensing, processing, actuation,

55
00:05:26,768 --> 00:05:32,741
and physical braking can contribute additional delay or distance
in a real system.

56
00:05:37,715 --> 00:05:46,377
Chapter five, Applications, explains real-time response. A
system must respond within a specified deadline.

57
00:05:46,547 --> 00:05:54,653
Real-time does not simply mean very fast. Correctness includes
whether the result arrives in time for its task.

58
00:05:54,823 --> 00:05:59,111
Missing a hard deadline can violate the system's requirements.

59
00:05:59,221 --> 00:06:02,784
Missing a soft deadline reduces quality of service.

60
00:06:02,954 --> 00:06:07,669
Moving a computation locally may remove some communication
delay,

61
00:06:07,779 --> 00:06:11,171
but does not automatically guarantee a safe outcome.

62
00:06:11,341 --> 00:06:15,671
Use this distinction when evaluating a remote controller:

63
00:06:15,781 --> 00:06:19,813
what is its deadline, and what happens if the response is late?

64
00:06:19,983 --> 00:06:26,319
A good average is insufficient when the important problem is an
occasional deadline miss.

65
00:06:26,489 --> 00:06:32,612
A deadline needs a defined start and finish. State whether you
measured just transmission,

66
00:06:32,722 --> 00:06:37,479
or sensing, queueing, computation, and the final response as
well.

67
00:06:42,411 --> 00:06:48,790
Match each response to a measured problem instead of choosing
the fastest-looking specification.

68
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:55,552
When capacity is limited, compress or summarise suitable data.
When round trips dominate,

69
00:06:55,662 --> 00:06:58,243
reduce unnecessary exchanges.

70
00:06:58,413 --> 00:07:05,837
Timestamps help detect stale measurements. A playback buffer can
smooth jitter, but the buffer adds delay.

71
00:07:06,007 --> 00:07:10,082
Reconnect logic and redundancy can improve resilience,

72
00:07:10,192 --> 00:07:15,952
while designed local control reduces dependence on an
unavailable remote link.

73
00:07:16,122 --> 00:07:21,370
Chapter six, Strengths and Limitations, reminds us that growth
adds load

74
00:07:21,480 --> 00:07:25,448
and coordination challenges, not just more devices.

75
00:07:25,618 --> 00:07:33,468
Every remedy has a trade-off. Diagnose, change one factor, and
measure again using the metric that matters.

76
00:07:37,715 --> 00:07:45,438
Your turn. At thirty metres per second, how far does an object
travel during a two hundred millisecond delay?

77
00:07:45,608 --> 00:07:52,072
Convert the units before multiplying. Take a few seconds, or
pause the video to work it out.

78
00:07:58,242 --> 00:08:05,367
Two hundred milliseconds is zero point two seconds. Thirty times
zero point two is six metres.

79
00:08:05,537 --> 00:08:12,172
This is only distance during the delay. It does not include the
rest of a real stopping process.

80
00:08:12,342 --> 00:08:14,667
Now consider a separate question:

81
00:08:14,777 --> 00:08:22,073
would adding a playback buffer always help a time-critical
controller? No, because buffering adds delay.

82
00:08:22,243 --> 00:08:28,046
The right network design depends on the task, not a single
universally best setting.

83
00:08:31,976 --> 00:08:35,795
Capacity is the limit, throughput is achieved transfer,

84
00:08:35,905 --> 00:08:40,726
latency is delay, jitter is variation, and loss is missing data.

85
00:08:40,896 --> 00:08:48,981
Our demonstration gave fifteen, one point five, and zero point
one five metres during three different delays.

86
00:08:49,151 --> 00:08:54,058
Those numbers illustrate timing; they are not total stopping
distances.

87
00:08:54,228 --> 00:09:01,183
Use the road analogy to keep the measures distinct, then replace
the analogy with an actual measurement.

88
00:09:01,353 --> 00:09:09,801
For practice, describe a slow application, name the metric you
would test, and justify one targeted improvement.

89
00:09:09,971 --> 00:09:16,307
Run the supporting delay calculator to check a second speed and
explain the units in your answer.

90
00:09:16,477 --> 00:09:23,069
Pip's late message is a timing problem first. More capacity may
help if a queue is the cause,

91
00:09:23,179 --> 00:09:26,912
but a measurement must connect the proposed change to that
cause.

92
00:09:31,454 --> 00:09:35,209
Pip's small problem now has an explanation you can test.

93
00:09:35,379 --> 00:09:41,160
Use Chapter 4, Networking, and the named subtopics to revisit
the reasoning.

94
00:09:41,330 --> 00:09:44,744
The tutorial guide includes the full teaching steps

95
00:09:44,854 --> 00:09:50,614
and the checked practice answers. Run the supporting
demonstration and change one input.

96
00:09:50,784 --> 00:09:56,117
Blue Impulse. Follow the signal, predict the result, and check
your explanation.
